'''Club Deportivo Leganés, S.A.D.''' is a football club based in [[Leganés]], [[Community of Madrid]], that currently competes in [[La Liga]], the first tier of the [[Spanish football league system|Spanish league system]]. In [[2024–25 La Liga|2024–25]], Leganés will play in [[La Liga]] by receiving automatic promotion after winning their first [[2023-24 Segunda División|Segunda]] title. It was founded on 23 June 1928. It holds home games at the [[Estadio Municipal de Butarque]], which seats 12,454 spectators.

==History==
The club was officially founded on 23 June 1928.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://as.com/futbol/2021/06/23/segunda/1624462021_272742.html|website=[[as.com]]|date=23 June 2021|title=El Leganés cumple 93 años apoyado en cinco pilares para crecer|first=J.|last=Martín|access-date=6 June 2022|archive-date=6 June 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220606203150/https://as.com/futbol/2021/06/23/segunda/1624462021_272742.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Its first president was Ramón del Hierro. However the club had to suspend operations in 1936 due to the Spanish civil war, where they remained inactive until they reformed on 4 September 1946.

Leganés played the vast majority of its existence in the lower leagues. In 1977 the club regained promotion to the [[Tercera División|fourth division]], where it had played before for seven years when the category was still the third level.

After a steady progression, Leganés reached the new [[Segunda División B|division three]] in 1987, being promoted to the [[Segunda División|second division]] six years later and maintaining its league status for 11 seasons; during this timeframe, it collected two consecutive eighth places (best) from 1995 to 1997.

On 24 December 2008, Victoria Pavón and Felipe Moreno acquired a majority stake of the club.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.marca.com/futbol/leganes/2019/12/24/5e01e217268e3e565e8b45ad.html|website=[[Marca (newspaper)|Marca]]|title=Once años de la llegada de 'Papa Noel' a Leganés|first=Javier|last=Carrasco|date=24 December 2019|access-date=6 June 2022|archive-date=6 June 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220606204431/https://www.marca.com/futbol/leganes/2019/12/24/5e01e217268e3e565e8b45ad.html|url-status=live}}</ref> Since July 2009, Victoria Pavón has been the president of the club.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.europapress.es/deportes/futbol-00162/noticia-futbol-leganes-presenta-nueva-presidenta-tecnico-luis-angel-duque-20090713220847.html|website=[[Europa Press (news agency)|Europa Press]]|date=13 July 2009|title=Fútbol.- El Leganés presenta a su nueva presidenta y al técnico Luis Ángel Duque|access-date=6 June 2022|archive-date=6 June 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220606204431/https://www.europapress.es/deportes/futbol-00162/noticia-futbol-leganes-presenta-nueva-presidenta-tecnico-luis-angel-duque-20090713220847.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.lavanguardia.com/deportes/futbol/20220308/8106005/victoria-pavon-machista-antipopular-intentan-delatarse.html|website=[[La Vanguardia]]|title=Victoria Pavón: "13 años después ya no es noticia que yo sea presidenta"|date=8 March 2022|first=Anaïs|last=Martí|access-date=6 June 2022|archive-date=6 June 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220606204431/https://www.lavanguardia.com/deportes/futbol/20220308/8106005/victoria-pavon-machista-antipopular-intentan-delatarse.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

In the [[2013–14 Segunda División B|2013–14 season]], Leganés promoted to [[Segunda División]] after 10 seasons in [[Segunda B]].
[[File:El CD Leganés a Primera (26911425003).jpg|thumb|The squad that earned promotion to La Liga in 2016 paying a visit to the regional government]]
[[File:Club Deportivo Leganés league performance 1929-2023.svg|thumb|Chart of CD Leganés league performance 1929-2023]]
In the [[2015–16 Segunda División|2015–16 season]], for the first time in their history, Leganés earned promotion to [[La Liga]], which was sealed on 4 June 2016 with a 1–0 away win against [[CD Mirandés]],<ref>{{cite news |last1=Plaza |first1=Víctor |title=El Leganés hace historia y asciende a Primera división |url=https://www.marca.com/futbol/segunda-division/2016/06/04/57533192e2704efa5b8b45f0.html |access-date=21 July 2020 |work=Marca |date=4 June 2016 |language=es |archive-date=15 June 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200615040502/https://www.marca.com/futbol/segunda-division/2016/06/04/57533192e2704efa5b8b45f0.html |url-status=live }}</ref> thus becoming the fifth team from [[Community of Madrid]] to ever play in La Liga after [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]], [[Atlético Madrid]], [[Rayo Vallecano]], and [[Getafe CF|Getafe]]. They remained in the top flight for four seasons, reaching a peak of 13th in [[2018–19 La Liga|2018–19]], before relegation in the last game of the following season, a 2–2 home draw with [[Real Madrid]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Lowe |first1=Sid |title=Silence, solitude and sadness for Leganés after desperate La Liga finale |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2020/jul/20/la-liga-leganes-relegated-silence-solitude-sadness-sid-lowe |author-link=Sid Lowe |access-date=21 July 2020 |work=The Guardian |date=20 July 2020 |archive-date=20 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200720203204/https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2020/jul/20/la-liga-leganes-relegated-silence-solitude-sadness-sid-lowe |url-status=live }}</ref> During this spell, the team qualified for the first time to the semifinals of the [[Copa del Rey]], by eliminating [[Real Madrid C.F.|Real Madrid]] in the [[2017–18 Copa del Rey#Quarter-finals|quarterfinals]] thanks to a 2–1 win at [[Santiago Bernabéu Stadium]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/jan/24/real-madrid-copa-del-rey-leganes-defeat|work=The Guardian|date=24 January 2018|access-date=25 January 2018|title=Real Madrid dumped out of Copa del Rey by Leganés at Bernabéu|archive-date=25 January 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180125054902/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2018/jan/24/real-madrid-copa-del-rey-leganes-defeat|url-status=live}}</ref>

In the [[2023–24 Segunda División|2023–24 season]], Leganes finished at first place in the Segunda Division and were promoted to La Liga, thus returning after a four season absence.
[[File:Ascenso a Primera 2024 CD Leganés.jpg|thumb|The squad that won La Liga in 2024 paying a visit to the regional government.]]
[[File:Deporleganes.jpg|thumb|Match between [[Deportivo de La Coruña]] vs CD Leganés in [[Teresa Herrera Trophy]] 2024.]]

==Fans==
The fans have friendly relation with [[ultras]] group ''Gate 12'' of [[Egaleo F.C.|Egaleo FC]], the towns of [[Egaleo]] and [[Leganés]] happen to be twinned too. They also have friendly ties with [[Reading F.C.]] of [[England]], partially due to sharing their blue and white colours. Their biggest rival is [[Getafe CF|Getafe]] with whom they contest the [[South Madrid derby]].

